<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Background and main results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic ball packing problem, which asks when a disjoint union of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic balls of prescribed sizes can be symplectically embedded into a fixed <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic ball, is similar in spirit but quite distinct in substance from its more famous Riemannian cousin, namely the sphere packing problem (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CS13</a>]</cite>).
Let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B^{2n}(R):=\left\{\pi\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}|z_{i}|^{2}\leq R\right\}\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>π</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R):=\left\{\pi\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}|z_{i}|^{2}\leq R\right\}\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ball of area <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. radius <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{R/\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{R/\pi})</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> denote its interior.
We endow these with the restriction of the standard symplectic two-form <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\operatorname{std}}=\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\operatorname{std}}=\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Classically, note that any symplectic embedding
<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>
must satisfy
<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{i=1}^{k}R_{i}^{n}\leq R^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{i=1}^{k}R_{i}^{n}\leq R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
since symplectic embeddings preserve the standard volume form <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{n!}\omega_{\operatorname{std}}^{\wedge n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>std</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{n!}\omega_{\operatorname{std}}^{\wedge n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first nontrivial result on symplectic ball packings appeared in Gromov’s foundational 1985 paper on pseudoholomorphic curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(Gromov’s two ball theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gro85</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},R_{2},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},R_{2},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, any symplectic embedding <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R_{1})\bigsqcup B^{2n}(R_{2})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R_{1})\bigsqcup B^{2n}(R_{2})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}+R_{2}&lt;R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}+R_{2}&lt;R</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, in dimension four many more refined symplectic packing obstructions for multiple balls were found.
Notably, in the special case of balls of equal sizes, the supremal fraction <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(B^{4},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(B^{4},k)</annotation></semantics></math> of the volume of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}:=B^{4}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}:=B^{4}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> that can be filled by a symplectic ball packing <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> was computed in the combined works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gro85</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>]</cite> to be:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\hline\cr k&amp;1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5&amp;6&amp;7&amp;8&amp;\geq 9\\
\hline\cr\rule{0.0pt}{10.76385pt}v(B^{4},k)&amp;1&amp;\tfrac{1}{2}&amp;\tfrac{3}{4}&amp;1&amp;\tfrac{20}{25}&amp;\tfrac{24}{25}&amp;\tfrac{63}{64}&amp;\tfrac{288}{289}&amp;1\\[2.15277pt]
\hline\cr\end{array}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="5.0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_l ltx_border_r"><mi>k</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>5</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>6</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>7</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mn>8</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r"><mrow><mtext><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:10.8pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>25</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mn>25</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>63</mn><mn>64</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>288</mn><mn>289</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0.337em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\hline\cr k&amp;1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5&amp;6&amp;7&amp;8&amp;\geq 9\\
\hline\cr\rule{0.0pt}{10.76385pt}v(B^{4},k)&amp;1&amp;\tfrac{1}{2}&amp;\tfrac{3}{4}&amp;1&amp;\tfrac{20}{25}&amp;\tfrac{24}{25}&amp;\tfrac{63}{64}&amp;\tfrac{288}{289}&amp;1\\[2.15277pt]
\hline\cr\end{array}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, building on various works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD98</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir01</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">LL01</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut11</a>]</cite>, the symplectic ball packing problem in dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n=4</annotation></semantics></math> has been entirely computed, or at least reduced to combinatorics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut11</a>, Rmk. 1.10]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if for each<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">In fact, using </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">LL02</a><span id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite><span id="footnote1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> it suffices to consider only certain distinguished tuples related to the Cremona group – see </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS12b</a><span id="footnote1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, §1.2]</span></cite><span id="footnote1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span> tuple of nonnegative integers <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> (not all zero) satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}(m_{i}^{2}+m_{i})\leq d^{2}+3d" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}(m_{i}^{2}+m_{i})\leq d^{2}+3d</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}&lt;dR." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}&lt;dR.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The obstructions involved in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition2" title="THEOREM 1.2 (see [, Rmk. 1.10]). ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> can be deduced either from exceptional curves in blowups of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, or using the embedded contact homology (ECH) capacities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Symplectic embedding problems are well-studied in dimension four, partly due to the existence of powerful gauge theoretic invariants like Seiberg–Witten theory, and partly thanks to useful tools for pseudoholomorphic curves in low dimensions such as positivity of intersections and automatic transversality.
For comparison, in higher dimensions we have the following conjecture concerning symplectic ball packings.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmlet1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjlet">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmlet1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONJECTURE A</span></span><span id="Thmthmlet1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmlet1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthmlet1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="Thmthmlet1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthmlet1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmthmlet1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmlet1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if the following hold:</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(volume obstruction) </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}^{n}+\cdots+R_{k}^{n}&lt;R^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}^{n}+\cdots+R_{k}^{n}&lt;R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(two ball obstruction) </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}+R_{j}&lt;R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}+R_{j}&lt;R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, for essentially formal reasons (based on a Fredholm index computation in §<a href="#S7" title="7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) there cannot be any stronger obstructions coming from pseudoholomorphic curves, at least under the standard paradigm for obstructing symplectic embeddings via closed or punctured curves.
In the spirit of Y. Eliashberg’s “holomorphic curves or nothing” philosophy, which posits that all nontrivial obstructions in symplectic geometry come from some kind of pseudoholomorphic invariants or classical invariants, we then expect higher dimensional ball packings to exhibit complete flexibility beyond the volume and two ball obstructions (see §<a href="#S7" title="7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> for more on what is currently known).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, we show that a certain stabilization procedure exhibits considerable rigidity for symplectic packing problems in higher dimensions. Fixing <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> denote a closed symplectic surface of genus <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and area <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a symplectic ball <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, we equip <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> with the product symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result is:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmlet2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thmlet">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmlet2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM B</span></span><span id="Thmthmlet2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmlet2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmlet2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a symplectic embedding <math id="Thmthmlet2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="Thmthmlet2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the same numbers appearing in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) give the supremal volume of <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> which can be filled by a packing <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> (for any fixed <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>).
We summarize the proof strategy for Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> in §<a href="#S1.SS3" title="1.3 Sketch of proof of main theorem ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, with the details appearing in §<a href="#S5" title="5 Proof of main theorem ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Using similar ideas as Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we also deduce a “stabilized” version of Gromov’s two ball theorem, which holds in any dimension:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmlet3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thmlet">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmlet3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM C</span></span><span id="Thmthmlet3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmlet3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmlet3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthmlet3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed
symplectic manifold which is symplectically aspherical.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Recall that a symplectic manifold </span><math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> is </span><span id="footnote2.12" class="ltx_text">symplectically aspherical</span><span id="footnote2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> if </span><math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> vanishes on </span><math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote2.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">. We expect this assumption could be removed at the cost of using virtual fundamental class techniques for Gromov–Witten theory.</span></span></span></span> Then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="Thmthmlet3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(B^{2n}(R_{1})\times N\right)\sqcup\left(B^{2n}(R_{2})\times N\right)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(B^{2n}(R_{1})\times N\right)\sqcup\left(B^{2n}(R_{2})\times N\right)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))\times N</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="Thmthmlet3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R_{1})\sqcup B^{2n}(R_{2})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R_{1})\sqcup B^{2n}(R_{2})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{2n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We give a general discussion of what our methods produce for higher dimensional stabilized problems in Remark <a href="#Thmremark9" title="Remark 9. ‣ 5.2 Proof of main theorem ‣ 5 Proof of main theorem ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Other stabilized embedding problems</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since ball packings are known to control various symplectic embeddings problems, we can use Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> to deduce analogous embedding results for stabilizations of other domains.
McDuff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite> showed that each symplectic embedding problem between four-dimensional rational ellipsoids is equivalent to some symplectic ball packing problem, where the sizes of the balls are read off from the area factors of the ellipsoids.
Cristofaro-Gardiner subsequently generalized this to a large class of four-dimensional toric domains as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>, Thm. 2.1]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a four-dimensional rational concave toric domain <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a four-dimensional rational convex toric domain <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists a ball packing</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(a_{i})\right)\sqcup\left(\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{\ell}B^{4}(b_{i})\right)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(b))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(a_{i})\right)\sqcup\left(\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{\ell}B^{4}(b_{i})\right)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(b)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},\dots,a_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},\dots,a_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight sequence of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b;b_{1},\dots,b_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b;b_{1},\dots,b_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is the negative weight sequence of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly recall the definitions of (rational) concave and convex toric domains and their weight sequences in §<a href="#S6" title="6 Stabilized embeddings for toric domains ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
The basic idea is that a four-dimensional concave toric domain canonically decomposes into a collection of symplectic balls, and similarly for the complement of a four-dimensional convex toric domain inside of a larger circumscribed ball.
By adapting this picture to the stabilizations <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{i}}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{i}}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and applying Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we get the following stabilized analogue of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition3" title="THEOREM 1.3 ([, Thm. 2.1]). ‣ Other stabilized embedding problems ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmlet4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thmlet">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmlet4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM D</span></span><span id="Thmthmlet4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmlet4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthmlet4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthmlet4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a four-dimensional concave toric domain, and let <math id="Thmthmlet4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a four-dimensional convex toric domain. Let <math id="Thmthmlet4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic surface of area <math id="Thmthmlet4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and genus <math id="Thmthmlet4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmlet4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with the main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>]</cite>, which states that the ECH capacities give sharp obstructions for embeddings of concave into convex toric domains,</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmlet5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corlet">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmlet5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">COROLLARY E</span></span><span id="Thmthmlet5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmlet5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthmlet5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="Thmthmlet5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}^{4},X_{\Omega_{2}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}^{4},X_{\Omega_{2}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet4" title="THEOREM D. ‣ Other stabilized embedding problems ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmlet5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if we have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{{\operatorname{ECH}}}_{k}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\leq c^{{\operatorname{ECH}}}_{k}(X_{\Omega_{2}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>ECH</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>ECH</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{{\operatorname{ECH}}}_{k}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\leq c^{{\operatorname{ECH}}}_{k}(X_{\Omega_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmlet5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmthmlet5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The ECH capacities of <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> have purely combinatorial descriptions – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>, §A]</cite> and the references therein for more details.
It is noteworthy that the ECH capacities control the six dimensional embedding problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ COROLLARY E. ‣ Other stabilized embedding problems ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), even though they are only defined in dimension four.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Specializing to ellipsoids <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E(a,b):=\{\pi|z_{1}|^{2}/a+\pi|z_{2}|^{2}/b\leq 1\}\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b):=\{\pi|z_{1}|^{2}/a+\pi|z_{2}|^{2}/b\leq 1\}\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (these are both concave and convex toric domains), embeddings of the form <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4})</annotation></semantics></math> were studied extensively in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS12b</a>]</cite>, where the function</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}}(x)=\inf\{\mu\;|\;E(1,x)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(\mu))\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}}(x)=\inf\{\mu\;|\;E(1,x)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(\mu))\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">was explicitly computed and shown to contain an “infinite staircase” controlled by the Fibonacci numbers. By Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet4" title="THEOREM D. ‣ Other stabilized embedding problems ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, this same function persists after stabilizing by <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmlet6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corlet">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmlet6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">COROLLARY F</span></span><span id="Thmthmlet6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmlet6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthmlet6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="Thmthmlet6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,x)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(\mu))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,x)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(\mu))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmlet6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmlet6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if we have <math id="Thmthmlet6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;f_{B^{4}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;f_{B^{4}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that there has been considerable recent interest in pushing four dimensional symplectic embedding results into higher dimensions by a different type of stabilization, namely taking products with <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, or more generally <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By analogy with the definition of <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> put</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x):=\inf\{\mu\;|\;E(1,x)\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(\mu))\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x):=\inf\{\mu\;|\;E(1,x)\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(\mu))\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">It is known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">HK14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGH18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGHM18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sie22</a>]</cite> that, for any <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq x\leq\tau^{4}:=\tfrac{7+3\sqrt{5}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq x\leq\tau^{4}:=\tfrac{7+3\sqrt{5}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x)=\tfrac{3x}{x+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x)=\tfrac{3x}{x+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for infinitely many values of <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau^{4},\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau^{4},\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">It is a subtle problem to determine which four-dimensional obstructions persist after stabilizing with <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math> and which do not. For instance, for <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large we have <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}}(x)=\sqrt{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}}(x)=\sqrt{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. volume is the only obstruction, while conjecturally (and definitely for certain values) we have <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x)=\tfrac{3x}{x+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B^{4}\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}}(x)=\tfrac{3x}{x+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, coming from Hind’s folding embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hin15</a>]</cite>.
By contrast, Corollary <a href="#Thmthmlet6" title="COROLLARY F. ‣ Other stabilized embedding problems ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> states that all four-dimensional obstructions persist after stabilizing with <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As another illustration of the difference between stabilizing with <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math>, let us note that in the latter case the analogue of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> is entirely flexible.
Namely, for any <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a symplectic embedding</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times\mathbb{R}^{2N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is a consequence of the fact that we can symplectically embed a disjoint union of finitely many copies of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math> into a single copy of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the condition <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math> is necessary by Gromov’s nonsqueezing theorem.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the embedding problems discussed above one could take the source to be either a compact domain or its interior, and similarly for the target, with the distinction between these choices being mostly academic (up to swapping inequalities with strict inequalities) since we are interested in <span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">optimal</span> embeddings.
For a more refined discussion see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD11</a>, Rmk. 1.3]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>, Cor. 1.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Open problems</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now mention a few open problems which seem to go beyond the techniques of this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">More general stabilizations by symplectic surfaces</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The stabilized packing problem addressed by Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> has the following natural extension.
For <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g_{1}}(L_{1}),\dots,\Sigma_{g_{k}}(L_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g_{1}}(L_{1}),\dots,\Sigma_{g_{k}}(L_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> denote closed symplectic surfaces with genera <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dots,g_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dots,g_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and areas <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},\dots,L_{k}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},\dots,L_{k}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QUESTION 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When does there exist a symplectic packing of the form</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g_{i}}(L_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g_{i}}(L_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))\times\Sigma_{g}(L),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for <math id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k},R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that by homology class considerations we must have <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}=\kappa_{i}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}=\kappa_{i}L</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard symplectic two-sphere normalized to have area <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
consider the embedding problem</p>
<table id="S1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(\kappa L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R^{\prime}))\times S^{2}(L)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(\kappa L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R^{\prime}))\times S^{2}(L),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for some fixed <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By taking a generic smooth perturbation of the composition of the degree <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> branched covering <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z^{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>κ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z^{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the inclusion <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{1}=\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times\{0\}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times B^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{1}=\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times\{0\}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times B^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, we produce a symplectic submanifold of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R^{\prime}))\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R^{\prime}))\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> having area <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the Weinstein neighborhood theorem, this extends to a symplectic embedding
<math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(\kappa L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R^{\prime})\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(\kappa L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R^{\prime})\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is interesting to ask how to construct such embeddings with maximal <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note the analogue of (<a href="#S1.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Example 1.5. ‣ More general stabilizations by symplectic surfaces ‣ 1.2 Open problems ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) in dimension four is vacuous, since symplectic embeddings of <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(\kappa L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(\kappa L)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> are prohibited by the adjunction formula.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Stabilizing by higher dimensional manifolds</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, the fact that <math id="S1.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is two-dimensional allows us to appeal to an h principle for symplectic surfaces in symplectic manifolds of dimension at least six (see <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition3" title="THEOREM 4.3 ([], see also []). ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).
As we explain in §<a href="#S4" title="4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, a similar h principle holds for higher dimensional symplectic submanifolds of codimension at least four, but it is no longer guaranteed that the relevant formal obstructions vanish.
Nevertheless, we can ask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QUESTION 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold of dimension <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Is it still true that <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times N\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times N\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times N</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Sketch of proof of main theorem</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We end this introduction by giving a brief outline of the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, while also outlining the main components of the paper.
The “if” direction is immediate, since a four-dimensional embedding can be stabilized to a six-dimensional one. The proof of the “only if” direction is based on the symplectic ball packing obstructions coming from exceptional curves in blowups of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>]</cite>.
It relies on the following three observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First we note that the existence of pairwise disjoint embeddings <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{i}:B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))\times\Sigma_{g}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{i}:B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B^{4}(R))\times\Sigma_{g}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> implies the existence of a symplectic blowup along the collection of symplectic surfaces <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)),\dots,\iota_{k}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)),\dots,\iota_{k}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> with respective sizes <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (here <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with the Fubini–Study form normalized so that a line has area <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>).
We denote this blowup by
<math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The theory of symplectic blowup along submanifolds is reviewed in §<a href="#S2" title="2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, with the aid of an h principle, we can symplectically isotope the collection of symplectic surfaces
<math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)),\dots,\iota_{k}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)),\dots,\iota_{k}(\{0\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> to a collection of fibers <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{1}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L),\dots,\{p_{k}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{1}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L),\dots,\{p_{k}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for some pairwise distinct <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{k}\in\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{k}\in\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then find a symplectic deformation equivalence between <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> and the size <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> blowup along the fibers <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{1}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L),\dots,\{p_{k}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{1}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L),\dots,\{p_{k}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
which we denote by <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> to be consistent with our later notation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we observe in §<a href="#S4" title="4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that the exceptional curves in blowups of <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> which give rise to packing obstructions in dimension four stabilize after crossing with <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, they are seen by Gromov-Witten invariants of <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence also of <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> by deformation invariance. Then the fact that the symplectic form must evaluate positively on these stabilized exceptional curves in <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> gives precisely the embedding obstructions of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Y.Y. would like to thank Dusa McDuff and Tian-Jun Li for helpful conversations. Y.Y. is partially supported by ERC Starting Grant No. 851701. K.S. is partially supported by NSF grant DMS-2105578.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we discuss the symplectic blowup construction along a symplectic submanifold, highlighting the main invariance properties we will need for the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
While the symplectic blowup at a point is well-studied (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, §7.1]</cite>), the analogue for symplectic submanifolds is somewhat less explored. Here we roughly follow the outline given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, §7.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After reviewing the blowup construction along a symplectic ball embedding <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extend this fiberwise to construct the symplectic blowup along an embedding <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma^{2n-2k}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma^{2n-2k}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The properties we will need of this construction are summarized in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>,
whose proof is provided in §<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>The symplectic blowup at a point</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to set up notation let us first briefly review the symplectic blowup at a point <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in a symplectic manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that this depends on a size parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a symplectic embedding <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Roughly speaking, we obtain a new symplectic manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by removing the interior of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(B^{2n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(B^{2n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> and collapsing its boundary along the fibers of the Hopf fibration to obtain an exceptional divisor <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq\widetilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq\widetilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> diffeomorphic to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here the blowup parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> translates into <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> having symplectic form <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\,\omega_{{\operatorname{FS}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>FS</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\,\omega_{{\operatorname{FS}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{{\operatorname{FS}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>FS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{{\operatorname{FS}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Fubini–Study form on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> normalized so that a line has unit area.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the complex analytic blowup of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at the origin (i.e. the total space of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(-1)\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(-1)\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>), with its natural projection maps <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{pr}}:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>pr</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{pr}}:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The exceptional divisor is <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=\pi^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=\pi^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we sometimes denote this by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}\subseteq\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}\subseteq\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> when no confusion should arise.
Given <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we equip <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with the symplectic form <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\omega}_{R}:=\pi^{*}\omega_{\operatorname{std}}+R\,{\operatorname{pr}}^{*}\omega_{{\operatorname{FS}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>std</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>pr</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>FS</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\omega}_{R}:=\pi^{*}\omega_{\operatorname{std}}+R\,{\operatorname{pr}}^{*}\omega_{{\operatorname{FS}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\operatorname{std}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\operatorname{std}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard symplectic form on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{B}^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{B}^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the preimage of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> under the projection <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, Lem. 7.1.11]</cite>, there is a diffeomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{R}:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Z\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{n}\setminus B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{R}:\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Z\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{n}\setminus B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> which restricts to a symplectomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\widetilde{B}^{2n}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z,\widetilde{\omega}_{R})\cong(B^{2n}(R+\varepsilon)\setminus B^{2n}(R),\omega_{\operatorname{std}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\widetilde{B}^{2n}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z,\widetilde{\omega}_{R})\cong(B^{2n}(R+\varepsilon)\setminus B^{2n}(R),\omega_{\operatorname{std}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose that we have a symplectic embedding <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this situation we define the symplectic blowup by first extending <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> to a symplectic embedding <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R+\varepsilon)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R+\varepsilon)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (c.f. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, Thm. 3.3.1]</cite>), and then putting</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}M:=\left(M\setminus\iota(B^{2n}(R))\sqcup\widetilde{B}^{2n}(\varepsilon)\right)/\sim," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}M:=\left(M\setminus\iota(B^{2n}(R))\sqcup\widetilde{B}^{2n}(\varepsilon)\right)/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where we identify each point in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{B}^{2n}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{B}^{2n}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z</annotation></semantics></math> with its image under <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\circ F_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\circ F_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can check that, up to symplectomorphism, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}M</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the extension of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R+\varepsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R+\varepsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and is also unchanged if we deform <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> through symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, Thm. 7.1.23]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now generalize the above discussion to symplectic blowups along symplectic submanifolds as follows.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic manifold and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{2n-2k}\subseteq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{2n-2k}\subseteq M</annotation></semantics></math> a symplectic submanifold of codimension <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>. We henceforth assume <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that the germ of the symplectic form near <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is determined up to symplectomorphism by the symplectic normal bundle over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, Thm. 3.4.10]</cite>).
In particular, when the symplectic normal bundle is trivial there is a small neighborhood of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which is symplectomorphic to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2k}(\delta)\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2k}(\delta)\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> (here we equip <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with the induced symplectic form <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega|_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega|_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2k}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2k}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\operatorname{std}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\operatorname{std}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DEFINITION 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD84</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{2n-2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{2n-2k}</annotation></semantics></math> be closed symplectic manifolds, and let <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math>
be a symplectic embedding for some <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the symplectic blowup along <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by first extending <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> to a symplectic embedding
<math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and then putting</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M):=\left(M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\sqcup(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\right)/\sim," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M):=\left(M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\sqcup(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\right)/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where we identify each point in <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with its image under <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\iota\circ(F_{R}\times{\operatorname{Id}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\circ(F_{R}\times{\operatorname{Id}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="DEFINITION 2.1 (see also []). ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> the symplectic submanifold <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\{0\}\times\Sigma)\subseteq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\{0\}\times\Sigma)\subseteq M</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily has trivial symplectic normal bundle.
We will refer to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> as a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">blowup along <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\{0\}\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\{0\}\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The construction in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="DEFINITION 2.1 (see also []). ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> also naturally generalizes to the case of pairwise disjoint embeddings <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{i}:B^{2k}(R_{i})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{i}:B^{2k}(R_{i})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> and some <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> (say by blowing up along the embeddings one at a time), and in this case we denote the corresponding blowup by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that there is a natural symplectomophism</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta:{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)\setminus(\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\Sigma)\rightarrow M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta:{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)\setminus(\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\Sigma)\rightarrow M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and this induces a homology-level isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{*}^{-1}:H_{2}(M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R))\times\Sigma)\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)\setminus(\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\Sigma))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{*}^{-1}:H_{2}(M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R))\times\Sigma)\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)\setminus(\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\Sigma)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">We also have an inclusion-induced maps <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma))\rightarrow H_{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(M\setminus\iota(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma))\rightarrow H_{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)\setminus(\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\Sigma))\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)\setminus(\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\Sigma))\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M))</annotation></semantics></math>. The first of these is an isomorphism provided that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case together with (<a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) we get a map <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(M)\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(M)\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M))</annotation></semantics></math> which we denote again by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This map is injective since it splits the projection map <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M))\rightarrow H_{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M))\rightarrow H_{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition lists the main properties we will need for the blowup construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the symplectic blowup along an embedding <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma^{2n-2k}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma^{2n-2k}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> as in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="DEFINITION 2.1 (see also []). ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(A)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M);\mathbb{R})\cong H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})\oplus\mathbb{R}E," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M);\mathbb{R})\cong H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})\oplus\mathbb{R}E,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whose inverse restricts to </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}^{-1}:H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M);\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}^{-1}:H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M);\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and sends </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to the homology class of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times\{{\operatorname{pt}}\}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\{{\operatorname{pt}}\}\subseteq\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\subseteq{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times\{{\operatorname{pt}}\}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}\times\{{\operatorname{pt}}\}\subseteq\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\subseteq{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The symplectic form on </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is characterized homologically by the property that the symplectic area of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}^{-1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}^{-1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> agrees with that of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and the symplectic area of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times{\operatorname{pt}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbb{CP}^{1}\times{\operatorname{pt}}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(B)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}:B^{2k}(R_{1})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}:B^{2k}(R_{1})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R_{2})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R_{2})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are two symplectic embeddings (for some </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},R_{2}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},R_{2}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) such that </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are isotopic through symplectic submanifolds in </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then the corresponding blowups </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M),\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M),\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M),\omega_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M),\omega_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are symplectically deformation equivalent in the following sense. For </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, let </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{i})_{*}^{-1}:H_{2}(M)\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{i}}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{i})_{*}^{-1}:H_{2}(M)\rightarrow H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{i}}(M))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denote the map on homology induced by </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and identify </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{i}}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{i}}(M))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(M)\oplus\mathbb{R}E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(M)\oplus\mathbb{R}E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> using (</span><a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Item (A) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exists a diffeomorphism </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)\rightarrow{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)\rightarrow{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{*}(E_{1})=E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{*}(E_{1})=E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{*}\circ(\beta_{1})_{*}^{-1}=(\beta_{2})_{*}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{*}\circ(\beta_{1})_{*}^{-1}=(\beta_{2})_{*}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is connected to </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by a 1-parameter family of symplectic forms.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Part <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item (A) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(A)</span></a> follows a Mayer-Vietoris sequence argument (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD84</a>, Prop. 2.4]</cite>).
Taking the linear dual to (<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) gives</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M);\mathbb{R})\cong H^{2}(M;\mathbb{R})\oplus\mathbb{R}e," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M);\mathbb{R})\cong H^{2}(M;\mathbb{R})\oplus\mathbb{R}e,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle e,E\rangle=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle e,E\rangle=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle e,A\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle e,A\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under this isomorphism, the symplectic form of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M;\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M;\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> lies in class <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\beta^{*}\omega]+Re" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\beta^{*}\omega]+Re</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\beta^{*}\omega],A\rangle=\langle[\omega],A\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\beta^{*}\omega],A\rangle=\langle[\omega],A\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in H_{2}(M;\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\beta^{*}\omega],E\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\beta^{*}\omega],E\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To explain the significance of <a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="Item (B) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(B)</span></a>, we observe that the formula for <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> given in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ DEFINITION 2.1 (see also []). ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) depends on the embedding <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> and not just its image.
Then given two embeddings <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1},\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1},\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> are isotopic as symplectic submanifolds in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, it is not a priori clear that <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectically deformation equivalent.
More specifically, given a smooth map <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)</annotation></semantics></math> which is not homotopic to a constant map, we can consider the symplectic embedding <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\chi}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\chi}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\chi}(a,b):=\iota(\chi(b)(a),b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\chi}(a,b):=\iota(\chi(b)(a),b)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then it is still not immediately clear that <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>ι</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{\chi}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{\chi}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectically deformation equivalent, even though <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\chi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\chi}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same images as embeddings <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\chi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\chi}</annotation></semantics></math> induce trivializations of the symplectic normal bundle of <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\{0\}\times\Sigma)=\iota_{\chi}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\{0\}\times\Sigma)=\iota_{\chi}(\{0\}\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which are (by construction) not homotopic through such trivializations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We take up the proof of <a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="Item (B) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(B)</span></a> in the next section. The idea is that given two symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, by appropriately shrinking the area parameter <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, we can deform <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> so that there is some <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}=\iota_{1\chi}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}=\iota_{1\chi}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we show explicitly <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}^{\prime}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}^{\prime}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1\chi}^{\prime}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1\chi}^{\prime}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectomorphic. The key idea in this step is that the standard symplectic form on <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(k)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant, so that the identity map <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}^{\prime}}(M)\setminus(\Sigma\times\mathbb{CP}^{k-1})\rightarrow{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1\chi}^{\prime}}(M)\setminus(\Sigma\times\mathbb{CP}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}^{\prime}}(M)\setminus(\Sigma\times\mathbb{CP}^{k-1})\rightarrow{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1\chi}^{\prime}}(M)\setminus(\Sigma\times\mathbb{CP}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to the entire blowup as a symplectomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section 7.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS12a</a>]</cite> it is explained how to take the symplectic blow up of a symplectic submanifold with nontrivial symplectic normal bundle (See also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD84</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS89</a>]</cite>). The conclusion of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> is still true for these more general blowups, however the proof of <a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="Item (B) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(B)</span></a> becomes more difficult to write down because one needs to keep track of all the choices made during the blowup construction. Since in this paper we only need the simplest case as described in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="DEFINITION 2.1 (see also []). ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we content ourselves to studying blowups in this simpler setting.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Deformation equivalence of the blowup</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove Part <a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="Item (B) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(B)</span></a> of proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
We start with</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{t}:B^{2k}(R_{t})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{t}:B^{2k}(R_{t})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> denote a smooth 1-parameter family of symplectic embeddings, of radius <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> also varies smoothly with <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>). Then the blowups <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are deformation equivalent in the sense of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We observe it suffices to construct a 1-parameter family of symplectic manifolds <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t},\omega_{t})_{t\in[1,2]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},\omega_{t})_{t\in[1,2]}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\omega_{1})={{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\omega_{1})={{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{2},\omega_{2})={{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{2},\omega_{2})={{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">To be precise, when we say a 1-parameter family of symplectic manifolds, we mean a fiber bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a smooth two form <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. We require the fiber <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t},\omega|_{X_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},\omega|_{X_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to be a symplectic manifold. After choosing a smooth fiber connection, i.e. a splitting of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TX</annotation></semantics></math> into into vertical and horizontal subspaces, on this bundle, this induces a parallel transport map <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{t}:X_{1}\rightarrow X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{t}:X_{1}\rightarrow X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we may consider instead a fixed smooth manifold <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> but a 1-parameter family of symplectic forms <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{t}^{*}\omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{t}^{*}\omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{2},\omega_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{2},\omega_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are deformation equivalent in the sense of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">To do this, consider <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times[1,2]_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times[1,2]_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We choose <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> small enough so that for all <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> the map <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a map <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{t}:B^{2k}(R_{t}+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{t}:B^{2k}(R_{t}+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply the construction in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="DEFINITION 2.1 (see also []). ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> in families. To be precise we cut out <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{t}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\times[1,2]\subseteq M\times[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{t}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\times[1,2]\subseteq M\times[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> and glue back in <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\times\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\times\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> using the map <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\iota_{t}\circ(F_{R_{t}}\times{\operatorname{Id}}),{\operatorname{Id}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\iota_{t}\circ(F_{R_{t}}\times{\operatorname{Id}}),{\operatorname{Id}}).</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given two symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1},\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1},\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}\quad d\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=d\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}\quad d\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=d\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then after shrinking <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> appropriately, there exists a one parameter family of symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{t}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{t}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> that starts at <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and ends at <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is modelled after proof of Theorem 3.3.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>]</cite>.
First choose a 1-parameter family of smooth embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{t},t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{t},t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{t}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma},\quad dj_{t}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=d\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{t}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma},\quad dj_{t}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=d\iota_{1}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then by the relative Poincaré lemma, there exists an one parameter family of one forms <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> so that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}|_{0\times\Sigma}=0,\quad d\alpha_{t}=j_{t}^{*}\omega-(\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma}),\quad\alpha_{0}=\alpha_{1}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}|_{0\times\Sigma}=0,\quad d\alpha_{t}=j_{t}^{*}\omega-(\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma}),\quad\alpha_{0}=\alpha_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Choose <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> small enough so that <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma}+sd\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma}+sd\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Then after shrinking <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to be small enough,
it follows from Moser isotopy there exists a 2-parameter family of embeddings</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{s,t}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\rightarrow B^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{s,t}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\rightarrow B^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">so that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{*}_{s,t}(\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma}+sd\alpha_{t})=\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma},\quad\chi_{s,t}|_{0\times\Sigma}={\operatorname{Id}},\quad\chi_{0,t}=\chi_{s,0}=\chi_{s,1}={\operatorname{Id}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{*}_{s,t}(\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma}+sd\alpha_{t})=\omega_{std}+\omega|_{\Sigma},\quad\chi_{s,t}|_{0\times\Sigma}={\operatorname{Id}},\quad\chi_{0,t}=\chi_{s,0}=\chi_{s,1}={\operatorname{Id}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">Our desired family of symplectic embeddings is then <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{t}\circ\chi_{1,t}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{t}\circ\chi_{1,t}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Given two symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1},\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1},\iota_{2}:B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> that agree on <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0\}\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0\}\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, we now proceed to show <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are deformation equivalent. Once we have this, Part <a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="Item (B) ‣ PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(B)</span></a> of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> follow directly from Propositions <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="PROPOSITION 2.4. ‣ 2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="PROPOSITION 2.3. ‣ 2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Observe the element <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\iota_{1}^{-1}\circ d\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\iota_{1}^{-1}\circ d\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> can be naturally viewed as a map <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{2}:\Sigma\rightarrow Sp(2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{2}:\Sigma\rightarrow Sp(2k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Take <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small. Consider the symplectic embedding <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}:B^{2k}(R^{\prime})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}:B^{2k}(R^{\prime})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> defined to be</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}(a,b):=\iota_{1}(\chi_{2}(b)(a),b)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}(a,b):=\iota_{1}(\chi_{2}(b)(a),b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in B^{2k}(R^{\prime})\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in B^{2k}(R^{\prime})\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> needs to be chosen small enough so that <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{2}(b)(a)\in B^{2k}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{2}(b)(a)\in B^{2k}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=d\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}=d\iota_{2}|_{\{0\}\times\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence Propositions <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="PROPOSITION 2.4. ‣ 2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="PROPOSITION 2.3. ‣ 2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> combine to show <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is deformation equivalent to <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{2}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, there exists a path of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{t}:\Sigma\rightarrow Sp(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{t}:\Sigma\rightarrow Sp(k)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and ending at <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> takes <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence by potentially shrinking <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> again we have a one parameter family of symplectic embeddings given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1,\chi_{t}}:B^{2k}(R^{\prime})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M,\quad\iota_{1,\chi_{t}}(a,b):=\iota_{1}(\chi_{t}(b)a,b)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1,\chi_{t}}:B^{2k}(R^{\prime})\times\Sigma\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M,\quad\iota_{1,\chi_{t}}(a,b):=\iota_{1}(\chi_{t}(b)a,b).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Using this 1-parameter family of symplectic embeddings, Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="PROPOSITION 2.3. ‣ 2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> tells us <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{2}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is deformation equivalent to <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof of proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> is then concluded by the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The symplectic manifolds <math id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectormorphic. Here <math id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}:=\iota_{1}(\chi_{1}(b)a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}:=\iota_{1}(\chi_{1}(b)a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{1}:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{1}:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first recall elements <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in U(k)</annotation></semantics></math> act symplectomorphically on <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\rightarrow gz\in\mathbb{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\rightarrow gz\in\mathbb{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a natural lifted action of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(k)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{k},\widetilde{\omega}_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{k},\widetilde{\omega}_{R})</annotation></semantics></math> also by symplectomorphisms. To see this, we recall we may write elements of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbb{C}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as pairs <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="([l],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([l],z)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l]</annotation></semantics></math> is a line through the origin and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is a point on <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\cdot([l],z)=([gl],gz)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\cdot([l],z)=([gl],gz)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{1}:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{1}:\Sigma\rightarrow U(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, the map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\chi_{1}}:B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\rightarrow B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma,\quad f_{\chi_{1}}(a,b):=(\chi(b)(a),b)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\chi_{1}}:B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\rightarrow B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\times\Sigma,\quad f_{\chi_{1}}(a,b):=(\chi(b)(a),b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a symplectomorphism. Likewise, the lifted map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}}:\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\rightarrow\widetilde{B}^{2k}\times\Sigma,\quad\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}}(([l],z),b):=\big(([\chi_{1}(b)l],\chi_{1}(b)z),b\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}}:\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma\rightarrow\widetilde{B}^{2k}\times\Sigma,\quad\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}}(([l],z),b):=\big(([\chi_{1}(b)l],\chi_{1}(b)z),b\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">is also a symplectomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F_{R},Id):\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z\times\Sigma\rightarrow B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\setminus B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F_{R},Id):\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z\times\Sigma\rightarrow B^{2k}(R+\varepsilon)\setminus B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(k)</annotation></semantics></math> equivariant symplectomorphism. In particular</span></p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\chi_{1}}\circ(F_{R},Id)=(F_{R},Id)\circ\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\chi_{1}}\circ(F_{R},Id)=(F_{R},Id)\circ\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">with domain of the above equation being <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now recall that <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by taking</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M):=\left(M\setminus\iota_{1}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\sqcup(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\right)/\sim," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M):=\left(M\setminus\iota_{1}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\sqcup(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\right)/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">where the equivalence relation is given by taking <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and gluing it to <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\iota_{1}\circ(F_{R}\times{\operatorname{Id}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}\circ(F_{R}\times{\operatorname{Id}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by taking the disjoint union</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M):=\left(M\setminus\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\sqcup(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\right)/\sim," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}}(M):=\left(M\setminus\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)\sqcup(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\right)/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the gluing is done by taking <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\times\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\times\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\setminus Z</annotation></semantics></math> and gluing it to <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\iota_{1}\circ f_{\chi_{1}}\circ(F_{R}\times{\operatorname{Id}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{1}\circ f_{\chi_{1}}\circ(F_{R}\times{\operatorname{Id}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then the map from <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{\chi_{1}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{\chi_{1}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the identity from <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus\iota_{1,\chi_{1}}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus\iota_{1}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus\iota_{1}(B^{2k}(R)\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. And it is given by <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}},{\operatorname{Id}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{f}_{\chi_{1}},{\operatorname{Id}})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\subseteq{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{\chi_{1}}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\subseteq{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{\chi_{1}}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\subseteq{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{B}^{2k}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma)\subseteq{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\iota_{1}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. The map is a symplectomorphism over each of the two glued pieces, and is well-defined by (<a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.3 Deformation equivalence of the blowup ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Stabilizing <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in H_{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in H_{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> an integral homology class. Let <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of genus <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic maps <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\mathbb{CP}^{1}\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\mathbb{CP}^{1}\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> lying in the homology class <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> marked points in the domain.
Let <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,k}(M,A;J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,k}(M,A;J)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the moduli space of unparameterized curves given by quotienting by the action of <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{PSL}}(2,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{PSL}}(2,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> via biholomorphic reparameterizations.
For a curve <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J)</annotation></semantics></math> we denote its image in <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,k}(M,A;J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,k}(M,A;J)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[v]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\omega_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\omega_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> be another closed symplectic manifold.
Let <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be compatible almost complex structures on <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and consider the split almost complex structure <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}\times J_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}\times J_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that for any fixed <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in N</annotation></semantics></math> there is an inclusion map</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J_{M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto\hat{v}_{p}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto\hat{v}_{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{p}(x):=(v(x),p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{p}(x):=(v(x),p)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{CP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{CP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the almost complex structure <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{M}\times J_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{M}\times J_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is split, every element of <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily constant in the second factor, i.e. of the form <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{M}_{0,k}(M,A;J_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Specializing to the case <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluating at the marked point gives a well-defined smooth map</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{\mathrm{ev}_{M}},\widetilde{\mathrm{ev}_{N}}):\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})\rightarrow M\times N." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>ev</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>ev</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{\mathrm{ev}_{M}},\widetilde{\mathrm{ev}_{N}}):\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})\rightarrow M\times N.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that all elements of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0,0}(M,A;J_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0,0}(M,A;J_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> are somewhere injective and regular.
Assume also that the almost complex structure <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is integrable near a fixed point <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the moduli space <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> is regular and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a regular value of the evaluation map <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathrm{ev}_{N}}:\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})\rightarrow N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>ev</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathrm{ev}_{N}}:\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})\rightarrow N</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ev}_{N}^{-1}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ev</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ev}_{N}^{-1}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth submanifold of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_{0,1}(M\times N,A\times[{\operatorname{pt}}];J_{M}\times J_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in which <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is integrable. For <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in N</annotation></semantics></math>, the pullback tangent bundle of <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> splits as <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{q}^{*}T(M\times N)=v^{*}TM\oplus\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{q}^{*}T(M\times N)=v^{*}TM\oplus\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (here <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim N=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim N=2n</annotation></semantics></math>).
The linearized Cauchy Riemann operator for <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the form</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\hat{v}_{q}}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\hat{v}_{q}^{*}T(M\times N))\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\hat{v}_{q}^{*}(T(M\times N))))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\hat{v}_{q}}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\hat{v}_{q}^{*}T(M\times N))\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\hat{v}_{q}^{*}(T(M\times N)))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\hat{v}_{q}^{*}T(M\times N))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\hat{v}_{q}^{*}T(M\times N))</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Sobolev completion of the space of vector fields, for some <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the almost complex structure is split, the linearized operator also splits as <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{M}\oplus D_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{M}\oplus D_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{M}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\hat{v}_{q}^{*}TM)\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\hat{v}_{q}^{*}(TM))),\quad D_{N}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\mathbb{C}^{n})\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\mathbb{C}^{n}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{M}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\hat{v}_{q}^{*}TM)\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\hat{v}_{q}^{*}(TM))),\quad D_{N}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\mathbb{C}^{n})\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\mathbb{C}^{n})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">In the above we have used <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the trivial <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> bundle over <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is already surjective by assumption. For <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is integrable, it suffices to consider its restriction <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\mathbb{C})\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\mathbb{C}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N}:W^{1,\ell}(S^{2},\mathbb{C})\rightarrow L^{\ell}(S^{2},\Omega^{0,1}(\mathbb{C}))</annotation></semantics></math>. The fact this operator is surjective follows directly from the regularity criterion for line bundles in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS12a</a>, Lem. 3.3.1]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">There is a map from <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{q}N\rightarrow\ker D_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{q}N\rightarrow\ker D_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to moving <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in N</annotation></semantics></math>. This map is bijective by noting the index of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{dim }N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>dim </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{dim }N</annotation></semantics></math>. This tells us that after requiring the curve <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{v}_{q}\}_{q\in N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{v}_{q}\}_{q\in N}</annotation></semantics></math> to pass through <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times\{p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times\{p\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting moduli space is still regular; and <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same Fredholm index as <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the second component of <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant, the group of holomorphic reparameterizations only acts on the first factor <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. After quotienting out by this group, we recover the analogous statements for the unparameterized moduli spaces.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition above can be recast in terms of a computation of Gromov-Witten invariants of the manifold <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular it can be seen as an instance of the product formula of Gromov-Witten invariants, for example as explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">HS22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GW_{g,k,A}^{M}:H_{*}(M)^{\otimes k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{g,k,A}^{M}:H_{*}(M)^{\otimes k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote Gromov-Witten invariant counting genus <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves in <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in homology class <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> marked points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">COROLLARY 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math> are all semi-positive. Assume all curves counted by <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GW_{0,0,A}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,0,A}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the conditions stated in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="THEOREM 3.1. ‣ 3 Stabilizing 𝐽-holomorphic curves ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H_{*}(M\times N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H_{*}(M\times N)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the homology class <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[M]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]\in H_{*}(M\times N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[M]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]\in H_{*}(M\times N)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="GW_{0,0,A}^{M}=GW_{0,1,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{M\times N}(\alpha)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>pt</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,0,A}^{M}=GW_{0,1,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{M\times N}(\alpha).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmremark5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We expect Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="THEOREM 3.1. ‣ 3 Stabilizing 𝐽-holomorphic curves ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to admit an analogue in the setting of punctured pseudoholomorphic curves, with <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> a completed symplectic cobordism and <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> a closed symplectic manifold. After stabilization the punctured holomorphic curves will have punctures asymptotic to Morse-Bott families of Reeb orbits. In this setting one can use Wendl’s automatic transversality for punctured curves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen10</a>]</cite> (take all punctures to be “free” along Morse-Bott families of Reeb orbits) in lieu of the regularity criterion from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS12a</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6</span></span><span id="Thmremark6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the genus zero assumption is essential in the above results. We expect higher genus curves will disappear after a generic perturbation because their indices are negative. To be precise, after stabilizing by a symplectic manifold of dimension <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fredholm index of a curve changes by <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(2-2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(2-2g)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="Thmremark6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> curve scenario is very curious; in the case of closed curves, suppose we start with a rigid <math id="Thmremark6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curve in <math id="Thmremark6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. After we stabilize with <math id="Thmremark6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> we do get an <math id="Thmremark6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim N</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional family of non-transverse <math id="Thmremark6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves, all of which are index zero. After we perturb we would expect a discrete family will survive the perturbation and become transverse curves. The number of such curves we expect to be counted by an obstruction bundle. We give a lower dimensional manifestation of this phenomenon below.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(S^{1}\times S^{1},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(S^{1}\times S^{1},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the two dimensional torus, with the standard almost complex structure. Then there exists exactly one genus one <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curve <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:T^{2}\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:T^{2}\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>, and this curve is transverse (assuming we can vary the domain complex structure). Now stabilize with a symplectic surface of genus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, which we write as <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma_{g},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma_{g},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now consider <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}\times S^{1}\times\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}\times S^{1}\times\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. With the split almost complex structure we see a 2-dimensional family of non-transverse index zero holomorphic tori parametrized by <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now think the ambient space <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}\times S^{1}\times\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}\times S^{1}\times\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> times the mapping torus of the identity map on <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now perturb the mapping torus of the identity map to the mapping torus of a small non-degenerate Hamiltonian <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> (with appropriate choice of almost complex structure). The curves in the homology class <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{2}]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{2}]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]</annotation></semantics></math> that survive are precisely those corresponding the degree 1 periodic orbits of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>; we have nonzero such counts; and this count is given by Taubes’ Gromov invariant, as explained in section 2.6 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>h-principles</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now state the main h-principle result we will need to use.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma_{g}(L),\omega_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma_{g}(L),\omega_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed symplectic surface of genus <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and area <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the symplectic manifold <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L),\omega_{FS}+\omega_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L),\omega_{FS}+\omega_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Fubini-Study form.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{i}:\Sigma_{g}(L)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{i}:\Sigma_{g}(L)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite collection of disjoint symplectic embeddings, each in the homology class <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\operatorname{pt}}]\times[\Sigma_{g}(L)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\operatorname{pt}}]\times[\Sigma_{g}(L)]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a symplectic isotopy <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}={\operatorname{Id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}={\operatorname{Id}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}\circ\iota_{i}(\Sigma_{g}(L))=\{p_{i}\}\times\Sigma_{g}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}\circ\iota_{i}(\Sigma_{g}(L))=\{p_{i}\}\times\Sigma_{g}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for a collection of disjoint points <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{i}\}\in\mathbb{CP}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{i}\}\in\mathbb{CP}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first give a proof via an abstract h-principle in the style of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">EM02</a>]</cite>; we then use a direct construction using tools from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BO16</a>]</cite> to give a proof for the case where <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math> and we replace <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>General h-principle proof</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first recall the (parametric) h principle for symplectic embeddings between symplectic manifolds <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q^{2k},\omega_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q^{2k},\omega_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Here by “symplectic embedding” we mean an embedding <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:Q\hookrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:Q\hookrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{*}\omega_{M}=\omega_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{*}\omega_{M}=\omega_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is called an “isosymplectic embedding” in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">EM02</a>]</cite>, which also considers the weaker notion of an embedding whose image is a symplectic submanifold.</span></span></span>
Given smooth manifolds <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, a smooth map <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:TQ\rightarrow TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:TQ\rightarrow TM</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monomorphism</span> if</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">there exists a smooth map <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:Q\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:Q\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in T_{p}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in T_{p}Q</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(v)\in T_{f(p)}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(v)\in T_{f(p)}M</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F|_{T_{p}Q}:T_{p}Q\rightarrow T_{f(p)}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F|_{T_{p}Q}:T_{p}Q\rightarrow T_{f(p)}M</annotation></semantics></math> is an injective linear map for each <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> gives a vector bundle homomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TQ\rightarrow f^{*}TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TQ\rightarrow f^{*}TM</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">EM02</a>]</cite>, we use the notation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{bs}}\,F:=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>bs</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{bs}}\,F:=f</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are further equipped with symplectic forms <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, we will say that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic form preserving</span> if <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F|_{T_{p}Q}:T_{p}Q\rightarrow T_{f(p)}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F|_{T_{p}Q}:T_{p}Q\rightarrow T_{f(p)}M</annotation></semantics></math> pulls back the linear two-form <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{M}|_{f(p)}\in\Lambda^{2}(T^{*}_{f(p)}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{M}|_{f(p)}\in\Lambda^{2}(T^{*}_{f(p)}M)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{Q}|_{p}\in\Lambda^{2}(T_{p}^{*}Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{Q}|_{p}\in\Lambda^{2}(T_{p}^{*}Q)</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DEFINITION 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A formal symplectic embedding from <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth embedding <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:Q\hookrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:Q\hookrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies the cohomological condition <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}[\omega_{M}]=[\omega_{Q}]\in H_{2}(Q;\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}[\omega_{M}]=[\omega_{Q}]\in H_{2}(Q;\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>,
together with a smooth family of monomorphisms <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}:TQ\rightarrow TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}:TQ\rightarrow TM</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{bs}}\,F_{t}=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>bs</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{bs}}\,F_{t}=f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}=df" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}=df</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic form preserving.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(Q,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(Q,M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of all symplectic embeddings of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\operatorname{formal}}(Q,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>formal</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\operatorname{formal}}(Q,M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of all formal symplectic embeddings of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that there is a natural inclusion map <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\mathcal{S}(Q,M)\hookrightarrow\mathcal{S}_{\operatorname{formal}}(Q,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>formal</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\mathcal{S}(Q,M)\hookrightarrow\mathcal{S}_{\operatorname{formal}}(Q,M)</annotation></semantics></math> which associates to a symplectic embedding <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> the formal symplectic embedding with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}=df" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}=df</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gro86</a>]</cite>, see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">EM02</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,\omega_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,\omega_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> any symplectic manifold such that we have <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q\leq\dim M-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q\leq\dim M-4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the inclusion map <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\mathcal{S}(Q,M)\hookrightarrow\mathcal{S}_{\operatorname{formal}}(Q,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>formal</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\mathcal{S}(Q,M)\hookrightarrow\mathcal{S}_{\operatorname{formal}}(Q,M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a weak homotopy equivalence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will be primarily interested in the following special case, which corresponds to injectivity on <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">COROLLARY 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,\omega_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,\omega_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> any symplectic manifold such that we have <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q\leq\dim M-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q\leq\dim M-4</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0},f_{1}:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0},f_{1}:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> be symplectic embeddings which are joined by a smooth isotopy <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}:Q\hookrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}:Q\hookrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose further that we have a smooth family of bundle monomorphisms <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{s,t}:TQ\rightarrow TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{s,t}:TQ\rightarrow TM</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{bs}}\,F_{s,t}=f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>bs</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{bs}}\,F_{s,t}=f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{s,i}=df_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{s,i}=df_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0,t}=df_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0,t}=df_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1,t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is symplectic form preserving for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be joined by a smooth family of symplectic embeddings.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that under the hypotheses of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition4" title="COROLLARY 4.4. ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> we automatically have <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}^{*}[\omega_{M}]=[\omega_{Q}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}^{*}[\omega_{M}]=[\omega_{Q}]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,\omega_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,\omega_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> any symplectic manifold, and assume that we have <math id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim M\geq 2\dim Q+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim M\geq 2\dim Q+2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0},f_{1}:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0},f_{1}:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> be symplectic embeddings which are homotopic as smooth maps. Then <math id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be joined by a smooth family of symplectic embeddings.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7</span></span><span id="Thmremark7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the assumption <math id="Thmremark7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim M\geq 2\dim Q+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim M\geq 2\dim Q+2</annotation></semantics></math>, general position considerations show that any smooth homotopy <math id="Thmremark7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}:Q\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}:Q\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, becomes a smooth isotopy after a small perturbation.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> denote the fiber bundle over <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{q,s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{q,s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,s,t)\in Q\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,s,t)\in Q\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the space <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(T_{q}Q,T_{f_{t}(q)}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(T_{q}Q,T_{f_{t}(q)}M)</annotation></semantics></math> of linear maps from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{q}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{q}Q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f_{t}(q)}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f_{t}(q)}M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subbundle whose fiber <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}_{q,s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}_{q,s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the space
<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom_{\omega}(T_{q}Q,T_{f_{t}(q)}M)\subseteq\hom(T_{q}Q,T_{f_{t}(q)}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>hom</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom_{\omega}(T_{q}Q,T_{f_{t}(q)}M)\subseteq\hom(T_{q}Q,T_{f_{t}(q)}M)</annotation></semantics></math> of symplectic form preserving linear maps.
Note that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{q,s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{q,s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to the space <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k)</annotation></semantics></math>-frames in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}_{q,s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}_{q,s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to the space <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> of symplectic <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k)</annotation></semantics></math>-frames in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can also check that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to the real Stiefel manifold <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2n)/O(2n-2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2n)/O(2n-2k)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}^{\omega}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}^{\omega}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to the complex Stiefel manifold <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(n)/U(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(n)/U(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LEMMA 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q^{2k},\omega_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q^{2k},\omega_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> be symplectic manifolds, and let
<math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0},f_{1}:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0},f_{1}:Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> be symplectic embeddings which are smoothly isotopic.
If the pair <math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-connected, then <math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmdefinition6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be joined by a smooth family of formal symplectic embeddings.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows by a standard obstruction theory argument.
It suffices to find a section <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:Q\times[0,1]^{2}\rightarrow E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:Q\times[0,1]^{2}\rightarrow E</annotation></semantics></math> which agrees with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\times[0,1]\times\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\times[0,1]\times\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\times\{0\}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\times\{0\}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and has image in <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\times\{1\}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\times\{1\}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fixing a CW structure on <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> cell-by-cell.
Firstly, for each <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-cell <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> we pick compatible trivializations of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This identifies the restriction of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}|_{C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}|_{C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then pick any extension of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}\cong[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}\times[0,1]^{2}\cong[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists since <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))=0</annotation></semantics></math> by assumption.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose by induction that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is already defined over all products of open <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-cells in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,\dots,i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,\dots,i-1</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq 2k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be an open <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-cell of <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. It suffices to extend <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By a deformation retract argument, we can assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is already defined in an open neighborhood of <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial C\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial C\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a slight shrinkening of <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> which is diffeomorphic to a closed <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-cell,
so that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is already defined near <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial C^{\prime}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial C^{\prime}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}\times\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}\times\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we can extend <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}\times[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}\times[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as long as <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i+1}({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i+1}({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which holds by assumption.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LEMMA 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The real frame manifold <math id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2n-2k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2n-2k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-connected and the symplectic frame manifold <math id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{Fr}}_{k}(\mathbb{C}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{Fr}}_{k}(\mathbb{C}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2n-2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2n-2k)</annotation></semantics></math>-connected. In particular, the pair <math id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}^{\omega}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}^{\omega}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmdefinition7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2n-2k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2n-2k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-connected.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first claim follows by an easy induction using fiber sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{i-1}\times{\operatorname{Fr}}_{i-1}(\mathbb{R}^{j-1})\rightarrow{\operatorname{Fr}}_{i}(\mathbb{R}^{j})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{j}\setminus\{\vec{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{i-1}\times{\operatorname{Fr}}_{i-1}(\mathbb{R}^{j-1})\rightarrow{\operatorname{Fr}}_{i}(\mathbb{R}^{j})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{j}\setminus\{\vec{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second claim similarly follows using the fiber sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2j-1}\times{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2i-2}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2j-2})\rightarrow{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2i}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2j})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2j}\setminus\{\vec{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2j-1}\times{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2i-2}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2j-2})\rightarrow{\operatorname{Fr}}_{2i}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{2j})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2j}\setminus\{\vec{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition5" title="PROPOSITION 4.5. ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim M=2n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q=2k</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Remark <a href="#Thmremark7" title="Remark 7. ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be joined by a smooth isotopy <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}:Q\hookrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}:Q\hookrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition6" title="LEMMA 4.6. ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> it suffices to check that <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}^{\omega}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Fr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\operatorname{Fr}}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}),{\operatorname{Fr}}^{\omega}_{2k}(\mathbb{R}^{2n}))</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-connected, and, since by assumption we have <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-2k-1\geq 2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-2k-1\geq 2k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, this follows by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition7" title="LEMMA 4.7. ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition1" title="PROPOSITION 4.1. ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> then follows from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition5" title="PROPOSITION 4.5. ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> and the isotopy extension theorem in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aur97</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>A direct construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we give an alternative proof of proposition <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition1" title="PROPOSITION 4.1. ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> in the special case that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> has genus zero, which we write as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the following proposition found in the appendix of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BO16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BO16</a>, Lem. A.5]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmdefinition8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> denote an open subset of <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which is diffeomorphic to a ball. Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2}:D(r)\rightarrow W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2}:D(r)\rightarrow W</annotation></semantics></math> be symplectic embeddings of a symplectic closed disc of area <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> that coincide on a neighborhood of the boundary. Then there is a compactly supported Hamiltonian function <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:W\times[0,1]\rightarrow W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:W\times[0,1]\rightarrow W</annotation></semantics></math> such that near a neighborhood of <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(\partial D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(\partial D)</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\cdot,t)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\cdot,t)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the time <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> flow of <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.Thmdefinition8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}\circ v_{1}=v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}\circ v_{1}=v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast to Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition3" title="THEOREM 4.3 ([], see also []). ‣ 4.1 General h-principle proof ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, which is based on the technique of holonomic approximation (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">EM02</a>, §3]</cite>), the proof of <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition8" title="PROPOSITION 4.8 ([, Lem. A.5]). ‣ 4.2 A direct construction ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> is very hands on and describes the Hamiltonian needed to realize this isotopy fairly explicitly.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8</span></span><span id="Thmremark8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The statement in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BO16</a>]</cite> it requires <math id="Thmremark8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> to be symplectomorphic to the standard symplectic ball, but upon inspecting the proof this assumption is not needed.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of genus zero version of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition1" title="PROPOSITION 4.1. ‣ 4 h-principles ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider first <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S^{2}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S^{2}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> a symplectic embedding in the homology class <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\operatorname{pt}}]\times[S^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\operatorname{pt}}]\times[S^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall first explain how to symplectically isotope this to the zero section of the form <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a point and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\varepsilon}(p)\subseteq S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\varepsilon}(p)\subseteq S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a disc of radius <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. We can, via a symplectic isotopy, assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\varepsilon}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\varepsilon}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\times D_{\varepsilon}(p)\subseteq B^{4}(R)\times D_{\varepsilon}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\times D_{\varepsilon}(p)\subseteq B^{4}(R)\times D_{\varepsilon}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next consider <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fiber of the trivial bundle <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}\rightarrow S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}\rightarrow S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and has homological intersection 1 with the image of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. We deform <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> by the Whitney trick so that its geometric intersection with the image of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is one and precisely at <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. During this deformation we assume the boundary of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> remains on <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the resulting submanifold by <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> denote a thickening of size <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{\prime}\ll\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≪</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{\prime}\ll\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)\cap 0\times S^{2}(L)=0\times D_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)\cap 0\times S^{2}(L)=0\times D_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then consider</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}:=B^{4}(R)\setminus\widetilde{F}_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}:=B^{4}(R)\setminus\widetilde{F}_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to the six ball (but not necessarily symplectomorphic). Then consider <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S^{2}(L)\setminus D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)\rightarrow W_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S^{2}(L)\setminus D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)\rightarrow W_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By A.5 there is a symplectic isotopy that takes <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{S^{2}(L)\setminus D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{S^{2}(L)\setminus D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\times S^{2}\setminus D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)\subseteq B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\times S^{2}\setminus D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)\subseteq B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> that fixes a neighborhood of the boundary. Since this isotopy fixes a neighborhood of the boundary we can extend it to all of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, and obtain an isotopy from <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{S^{2}(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{S^{2}(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This symplectic isotopy may have self intersections, in particular interior intersections with <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\times D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\times D_{2\varepsilon^{\prime}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>, but we can perturb them away generically. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is simply connected, we can upgrade this isotopy of symplectic embeddings to an ambient Hamiltonian isotopy, supported away from <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{4}(R)\times S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove the result for a collection of disjointly symplectically embedded <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>s, we observe we can perturb the isotopies of symplectic embeddings so that the isotopies of embedded symplectic spheres don’t intersect each other. For spheres all symplectic isotopies are Hamiltonian, so we can extend the family of symplectic embeddings to an ambient Hamiltonian flow.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Proof of main theorem</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we assemble the previous ingredients to prove our main theorem. As preparation, we first give a review of symplectic ball packings in dimension four.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Review of ball packings in dimension four</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a collection of points <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{k}\in\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{k}\in\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the corresponding complex blowup at these points by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the line class and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\dots,E_{k}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\dots,E_{k}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> the homology classes of the corresponding exceptional divisors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-K:=3A-\sum_{i}E_{i}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-K:=3A-\sum_{i}E_{i}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the anticanonical class. We define <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{K}\subseteq H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{K}\subseteq H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set of homology classes <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\cdot F=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\cdot F=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cdot F=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cdot F=-1</annotation></semantics></math> that can be represented by a smoothly embedded sphere in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DEFINITION 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD11</a>, Prop 3.2]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, Def. 1.3.A]</cite>)<span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A vector <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,m_{1},...,m_{k})\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\times\mathbb{Z}^{k}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,m_{1},...,m_{k})\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\times\mathbb{Z}^{k}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is called exceptional if the homology class <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dA-\sum_{i}^{k}m_{i}E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dA-\sum_{i}^{k}m_{i}E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROPOSITION 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD11</a>, Prop 3.2]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, Thm 1.3.C]</cite>)<span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that a symplectic packing of <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls of area <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> exists, then for each exceptional vector <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,m_{1},\cdots,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,m_{1},\cdots,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> we have the inequality</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}&lt;dR." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}&lt;dR.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In fact, the set of obstructions coming from exceptional vectors is equivalent to the combinatorial obstructions listed in <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition2" title="THEOREM 1.2 (see [, Rmk. 1.10]). ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, and hence form a system of sharp ball packing obstructions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the obstructions proceed as follows. Suppose such a ballpacking <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}CP^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}CP^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> exists, this induces a symplectic blow up of radius <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> around each embedded ball. We denote this blow up by <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>.The blown up symplectic form on <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> has first Chern class Poincare dual to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomology class <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\alpha+\sum_{i}R_{i}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\alpha+\sum_{i}R_{i}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means each exceptional rational curve can be represented by a rational symplectic sphere (Lemma 3.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">LL01</a>]</cite>, see also Theorem C in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">LL02</a>]</cite>), and knowing the area of a symplectic sphere is positive gives the constraints above.
Through the work of McDuff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD11</a>, Prop. 3.2]</cite>, the set of obstructions coming from exceptional vectors are in fact equivalent to the obstructions presented in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition2" title="THEOREM 1.2 (see [, Rmk. 1.10]). ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now record some properties of the exceptional curves from which the embedding obstruction arises. The description below follows from the exposition in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, Prop. 2.3.A]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denote an exceptional symplectic sphere on <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> in the homology class given by the exceptional vector <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,m_{1},...,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,m_{1},...,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> a generic compatible almost complex structure chosen so that <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic. Then as explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>]</cite>, the curve <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid, regular,
and unique in its homology class. Hence it follows that</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LEMMA 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,m_{1},\dots,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,m_{1},\dots,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be an exceptional vector and <math id="S5.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding homology class in <math id="S5.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GW^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))}_{0,0,[C]}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))}_{0,0,[C]}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Proof of main theorem</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small, consider the symplectic embedding <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(\varepsilon)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(\varepsilon)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> with the centers of the balls given by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{i}\}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{i}\}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting blow up. Recall second homology group of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> as a vector space over <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)))=\mathbb{R}A\oplus\bigoplus_{i=1}^{k}\mathbb{R}E_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)))=\mathbb{R}A\oplus\bigoplus_{i=1}^{k}\mathbb{R}E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consider the embedding <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{j}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{j}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(\varepsilon)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is given by the extension of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> by identity in the second component. Consider the space <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. This space is the same as the size <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> blowup of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{i}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{i}\}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we denote by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>. The second cohomology of this space is given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)))=\mathbb{R}\alpha\bigoplus\bigoplus_{i=1}^{k}\mathbb{R}e_{i}\bigoplus\mathbb{R}\omega_{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}\times\Sigma_{g}(L)))=\mathbb{R}\alpha\bigoplus\bigoplus_{i=1}^{k}\mathbb{R}e_{i}\bigoplus\mathbb{R}\omega_{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the cohomology class of the pullback of the Fubini-Study form on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> to the blowup <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> (see Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). We normalize it so that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\mathbb{CP}^{1}],\alpha\rangle=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\mathbb{CP}^{1}],\alpha\rangle=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{g}(L)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{g}(L)]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the fundamental class of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> under inclusion, we have the normalization <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\Sigma_{g}(L)],\omega_{L}\rangle=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\Sigma_{g}(L)],\omega_{L}\rangle=L</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting blown up symplectic form on this space has cohomology class given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\alpha]+\sum\varepsilon[e_{i}]+[\omega_{L}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\alpha]+\sum\varepsilon[e_{i}]+[\omega_{L}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to show for any exceptional vector gives rise to the same obstruction as Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 5.2 ([, Prop 3.2] or [, Thm 1.3.C]). ‣ 5.1 Review of ball packings in dimension four ‣ 5 Proof of main theorem ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose we have stabilized embeddings <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{4}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. We can view this as collection of symplectic embeddings into <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. We form the size <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> blowup and denote the resulting space by <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim<span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: The symplectic manifolds <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> are deformation equivalent. The symplectic form on <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math> lives in the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\alpha]+\sum R_{i}[e_{i}]+[\omega_{L}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\alpha]+\sum R_{i}[e_{i}]+[\omega_{L}].</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first part of the claim follows from the second part of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, and the second claim follows from the first part of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="PROPOSITION 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Blowing up a symplectic submanifold with trivial normal bundle ‣ 2 Blowing up along symplectic submanifolds ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To finish the proof,
consider an exceptional curve <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the exceptional vector <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,m_{1},...,m_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,m_{1},...,m_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>. By lemma <a href="#S5.Thmdefinition3" title="LEMMA 5.3. ‣ 5.1 Review of ball packings in dimension four ‣ 5 Proof of main theorem ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GW_{0,0,[C]}^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,0,[C]}^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R))}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="COROLLARY 3.2. ‣ 3 Stabilizing 𝐽-holomorphic curves ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> implies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="GW_{0,1,[C]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]}^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))}([{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}])=\pm 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,1,[C]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]}^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\hat{j}_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))}([{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{j_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}])=\pm 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since Gromov Witten invariants are preserved by deformations of the symplectic form, this means the same Gromov Witten invariant is nonzero for <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}(\mathbb{CP}^{2}(R)\times\Sigma_{g}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>. Measuring this Gromov-Witten invariant against the cohomology class</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\alpha]+\sum R_{i}[e_{i}]+[\omega_{L}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\alpha]+\sum R_{i}[e_{i}]+[\omega_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">and noting the fact the integral of a symplectic form on any <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves is positive, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum m_{i}R_{i}&lt;dR." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum m_{i}R_{i}&lt;dR.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 9</span></span><span id="Thmremark9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmremark9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denote any closed symplectic manifold.
Assume there is a version of Gromov-Witten invariants for which the conclusion of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="COROLLARY 3.2. ‣ 3 Stabilizing 𝐽-holomorphic curves ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
holds without any assumption on semi-positivity.
Consider symplectic embeddings <math id="Thmremark9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times N\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}B^{4}(R)\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\times N\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}B^{4}(R)\times N</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmremark9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}0\times N)\subseteq{\operatorname{int}}B^{4}(R)\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}0\times N)\subseteq{\operatorname{int}}B^{4}(R)\times N</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectically isotopic to the symplectic submanifold <math id="Thmremark9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}\{p_{i}\}\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}\{p_{i}\}\times N</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it follows from the techniques of this paper that such symplectic embedding is possible if and only if there is a symplectic embedding <math id="Thmremark9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}B^{4}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}B^{4}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To prove this, the only difference from the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> is the lack of an h-principle, but this is provided by the symplectic isotopy of the blowup locus stated here as an assumption. The existence of holomorphic curves is provided by Gromov-Witten invariants.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Stabilized embeddings for toric domains</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the moment map <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:\mathbb{C}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:\mathbb{C}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{1},z_{2})\rightarrow(\pi|z_{1}|^{2},\pi|z_{2}|^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{1},z_{2})\rightarrow(\pi|z_{1}|^{2},\pi|z_{2}|^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> denote a region in <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a <em id="S6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">toric domain</em> <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the pre-image of <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DEFINITION 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A toric domain <math id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is concave if <math id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the region in the first quadrant under the graph of a convex function <math id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,a]\rightarrow[0,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,a]\rightarrow[0,b]</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(a)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DEFINITION 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A toric domain <math id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex if <math id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed region in the first quadrant bounded by the coordinate axes and a convex curve connecting <math id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S6.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DEFINITION 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say a toric domain <math id="S6.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is rational, if <math id="S6.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> has upper boundary that is piecewise linear with rational slopes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a rational convex or concave toric domain, we can associate to it a finite sequence of numbers called a <em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weight sequence</em>. For details of this construction we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>]</cite>. The point is that for a concave toric domain we can associate to it a sequence of real numbers potentially with repetitions <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\Omega)=(a_{1},a_{2},...)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\Omega)=(a_{1},a_{2},...)</annotation></semantics></math>. Associated to this sequence of numbers is a collection of symplectic balls, which we write as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(\Omega)=\bigsqcup_{i}B^{4}(a_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(\Omega)=\bigsqcup_{i}B^{4}(a_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For convex toric domains, the weight sequence <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> takes the form <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b;b_{1},b_{2}...)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b;b_{1},b_{2}...)</annotation></semantics></math>. The first number <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is called the head, and the remaining un-ordered sequence of numbers is called the negative weight sequence. Associated to the negative weight sequence is a collection of symplectic balls of the form</p>
<table id="S6.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{B}(\Omega)=\bigsqcup_{i}B^{4}(b_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{B}(\Omega)=\bigsqcup_{i}B^{4}(b_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">THEOREM 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>, Thm 2.1]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational concave toric domain, and let <math id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational convex toric domain. Using the notation for weight sequences as above,
there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1}))\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2}))\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1}))\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2}))\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet4" title="THEOREM D. ‣ Other stabilized embedding problems ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to prove the only if direction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1<span id="S6.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. We first prove it for the case where both <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> are rational. Suppose we have a symplectic embedding</span></span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">If we decompose both <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the weight sequence, we obtain stabilized (open) ball packings</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then for any <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists packings</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(B(\lambda\Omega_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\bigsqcup(\hat{B}(\lambda\Omega_{2}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B(\lambda\Omega_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\bigsqcup(\hat{B}(\lambda\Omega_{2}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Here we use <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\Omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\Omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the toric domain rescaled by <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by
Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet2" title="THEOREM B. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> then implies there exists symplectic embeddings</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\lambda\Omega_{1})\bigsqcup\hat{B}(\lambda\Omega_{2}))\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B(b_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\lambda\Omega_{1})\bigsqcup\hat{B}(\lambda\Omega_{2}))\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B(b_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. As explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, this means <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1}))\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2})))\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B(b_{1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1}))\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2})))\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B(b_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math>
which implies a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{1}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega_{2}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2<span id="S6.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. To deduce the general case, suppose <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is any concave toric domain and <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is any convex toric domain, with an embedding of the form</span></span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(B(\Omega_{1})\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\bigsqcup{\operatorname{int}}(\hat{B}(\Omega_{2}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(B(b_{1}))\times\Sigma_{g}(L).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">The for any rational concave toric domain <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{\prime}\subseteq\Omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{\prime}\subseteq\Omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any rational convex toric domain <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{\prime\prime}\supset\Omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{\prime\prime}\supset\Omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the previous set implies we have the symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega^{\prime}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega^{\prime\prime}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega^{\prime}})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}(X_{\Omega^{\prime\prime}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then the conclusion of our theorem follows as in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG19</a>, Thm. 1.2]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Higher dimensional ball packings</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To motivate the ball packing problem we first give a review of the proof of Gromov’s two ball theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition1" title="THEOREM 1.1 (Gromov’s two ball theorem []). ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a symplectic embedding <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B^{2n}(R_{1})\bigsqcup B^{2n}(R_{2})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B^{2n}(R_{1})\bigsqcup B^{2n}(R_{2})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}{\operatorname{int}}B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. We compactify the target <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{int}}B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{int}}B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the corresponding blowup, <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that we have a natural identification <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))\cong\mathbb{Z}\langle A,E_{1},E_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))\cong\mathbb{Z}\langle A,E_{1},E_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the line class and <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},E_{2}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i},R_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},E_{2}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{p_{i},R_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> denote the homology classes of lines in the resulting exceptional divisors.
Recall that the Gromov–Witten invariant <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GW^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)}_{0,0,A-E_{1}-E_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)}_{0,0,A-E_{1}-E_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero (essentially since there is a unique line through any two points in projective space). <span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              To be more precise, since this blowup is not necessarily semi-positive, some additional technology is needed to make sure this is well defined. In the case of the Gromov two ball theorem the proof can be carried out entirely without blow up, however to be compatible with the formal calculations we provide below for Conjecture <a href="#Thmthmlet1" title="CONJECTURE A. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, we phrase everything in terms of the blown up projective space.</span></span></span>
In particular, for any tame almost complex structure <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, the compactified moduli space of stable maps
<math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),A-E_{1}-E_{2};J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),A-E_{1}-E_{2};J)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, and the energy of any curve <math id="S7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),A-E_{1}-E_{2};J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),A-E_{1}-E_{2};J)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\mathcal{E}(C)=R-R_{1}-R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\mathcal{E}(C)=R-R_{1}-R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic embedding of the form given in Conjecture <a href="#Thmthmlet1" title="CONJECTURE A. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, and let <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding symplectic blowup of <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points.
For nonnegative integers <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,m_{1},\dots,m_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,m_{1},\dots,m_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, put</p>
<table id="S7.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k}):=dR-\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k}):=dR-\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}R_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which represents the energy of a hypothetical pseudoholomorphic curve in <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> in homology class <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]:=dA-\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}E_{i}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]:=dA-\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}E_{i}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, for <math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> put</p>
<table id="S7.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k}):=(n-3)(2-2g)+2(n+1)d-2(n-1)\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ind</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k}):=(n-3)(2-2g)+2(n+1)d-2(n-1)\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which represents the (real) Fredholm index of a hypothetical curve in <math id="S7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in homology class <math id="S7.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]</annotation></semantics></math> (without any further constraints imposed).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LEMMA 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\vec{a}}=(a_{1},\dots,a_{k})\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\vec{a}}=(a_{1},\dots,a_{k})\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose that we have:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ind</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}+R_{j}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}+R_{j}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then we have <math id="S7.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume without loss of generality that we have <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\geq\cdots\geq a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\geq\cdots\geq a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using <a href="#S7.I1.i1" title="Item (a) ‣ LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(a)</span></a> we have</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}\leq\frac{n-3+(n+1)d}{n-1}\leq 2d," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}\leq\frac{n-3+(n+1)d}{n-1}\leq 2d,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">and hence using <a href="#S7.I1.i2" title="Item (b) ‣ LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(b)</span></a> and <a href="#S7.I1.i3" title="Item (c) ‣ LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(c)</span></a> we have</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq dR-dR_{1}-dR_{2}\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq dR-dR_{1}-dR_{2}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmdefinition1" title="LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> says roughly that any pseudoholomorphic curve bound obtained as in the above proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition1" title="THEOREM 1.1 (Gromov’s two ball theorem []). ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> cannot improve upon the bound coming from Gromov’s two ball theorem (applied to each pair <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math>).
Indeed, to see this, consider a homology class of the form <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]:=dA-\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}E_{i}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]:=dA-\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}E_{i}\in H_{2}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ind</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose we knew that for any symplectic embedding <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a tame almost complex structure <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> so that the compactified moduli space <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),[C];J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),[C];J)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty.
For example, this holds if the absolute Gromov–Witten invariant <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GW^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)}_{0,0,[C]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW^{{{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)}_{0,0,[C]}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero, or more generally if there is a nonvanishing Gromov–Witten invariant relative to the exceptional divisors in the homology class <math id="S7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, given any ball packing <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i}^{\prime})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i}^{\prime})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, by energy considerations as in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition1" title="THEOREM 1.1 (Gromov’s two ball theorem []). ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> we must have <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}^{\prime}\leq dR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}m_{i}R_{i}^{\prime}\leq dR</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, since we can always find such a ball packing with <math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}^{\prime}=R-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}^{\prime}=R-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small, we must have <math id="S7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\varepsilon)m_{1}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\varepsilon)m_{1}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we must have <math id="S7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{2},\dots,m_{k}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{2},\dots,m_{k}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <a href="#S7.I1.i3" title="Item (c) ‣ LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(c)</span></a> in Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmdefinition1" title="LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> holds by Gromov’s two ball theorem.
Then Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmdefinition1" title="LEMMA 7.1. ‣ 7 Higher dimensional ball packings ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> says that the energy of any curve <math id="S7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),[C];J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,0}({{\operatorname{Bl}}}_{\{\iota_{i}\}}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R),[C];J)</annotation></semantics></math> already satisfies <math id="S7.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(C)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(C)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. we do not obtain any further obstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The above discussion also easily extends to punctured curves say in the symplectic completion of the complementary symplectic cobordism <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:=B^{2n}(R)\setminus\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}\iota({\operatorname{int}}B^{2n}(R_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:=B^{2n}(R)\setminus\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}\iota({\operatorname{int}}B^{2n}(R_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> of a ball packing <math id="S7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{2n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R_{i})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B^{2n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Namely, put <math id="S7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{+}X:=\partial B^{2n}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{+}X:=\partial B^{2n}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{-}X:=\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}\iota(\partial B^{2n}(R_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{-}X:=\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}\iota(\partial B^{2n}(R_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}=X\cup(\partial^{+}X\times\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})\cup(\partial^{-}X\times\mathbb{R}_{\leq 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}=X\cup(\partial^{+}X\times\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})\cup(\partial^{-}X\times\mathbb{R}_{\leq 0})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the symplectic completion of <math id="S7.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Strictly speaking the contact boundaries <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\pm}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\pm}X</annotation></semantics></math> have Morse–Bott Reeb dynamics, but if desired we can slightly perturb each round ball into an irrational ellipsoid, in which case the same discussion carries over with minor adjustments.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Given a finite energy punctured curve <math id="S7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can associate to each puncture the multiplicity of the corresponding Reeb orbit.
Let <math id="S7.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denote the summed multiplicity of all punctures which are asymptotic to Reeb orbits in <math id="S7.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{+}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{+}X</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, for <math id="S7.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S7.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the summed multiplicity of all punctures which are asymptotic to Reeb orbits in the <math id="S7.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th boundary component of <math id="S7.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{-}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{-}X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then it is easy to check that the Fredholm index and energy of <math id="S7.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S7.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\operatorname{ind}}(C)\leq{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ind</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\operatorname{ind}}(C)\leq{\operatorname{ind}}_{g}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(C)=\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(C)=\mathcal{E}_{\vec{a}}(d;m_{1},\dots,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The association of values <math id="S7.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,m_{1},\dots,m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,m_{1},\dots,m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to a curve <math id="S7.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> naturally extends to elements of the SFT compactification by pseudoholomorphic buildings.
The upshot is that given any mechanism for producing punctured curves in <math id="S7.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with nonnegative index which insensitive to the sizes <math id="S7.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding inequality given by applying Stokes’ theorem cannot beat Gromov’s two ball theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Specializing to balls of equal size, note that for an embedding <math id="S7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(R)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the two ball obstruction gives <math id="S7.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so Conjecture <a href="#Thmthmlet1" title="CONJECTURE A. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> amounts in this case to:</p>
<table id="S7.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v(\mathbb{CP}^{n},k)=\begin{cases}\tfrac{k}{2^{n}}&amp;k=1,\dots,2^{n-1}\\
1&amp;k\geq 2^{n}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mfrac><mi>k</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v(\mathbb{CP}^{n},k)=\begin{cases}\tfrac{k}{2^{n}}&amp;k=1,\dots,2^{n-1}\\
1&amp;k\geq 2^{n}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p9.2" class="ltx_p">This holds for <math id="S7.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, since by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>]</cite> we have <math id="S7.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\mathbb{CP}^{n},k^{n})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbb{CP}^{n},k^{n})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in particular we have an embedding <math id="S7.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(1/2-\varepsilon)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup\limits_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(1/2-\varepsilon)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S7.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, it was also shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH11</a>]</cite> that <math id="S7.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\mathbb{CP}^{n},k)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbb{CP}^{n},k)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq M_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq M_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we can take <math id="S7.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n}=\lceil(8\tfrac{1}{36})^{\tfrac{n}{2}}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>36</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></msup><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n}=\lceil(8\tfrac{1}{36})^{\tfrac{n}{2}}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> (c.f. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS17</a>, Rmk. 7.1.31]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Stabilized Gromov two ball theorem</h2>

<div id="S8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthmlet3" title="THEOREM C. ‣ 1.1 Background and main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider the two embeddings given by <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:B^{2n}(R_{i})\times N\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:B^{2n}(R_{i})\times N\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> denote the homology class <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbb{CP}^{n}]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbb{CP}^{n}]\times[{\operatorname{pt}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the point class in <math id="S8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n}\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n}\times N</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a straightforward generalization of theorem <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="THEOREM 3.1. ‣ 3 Stabilizing 𝐽-holomorphic curves ‣ On Symplectic Packing Problems in Higher Dimensions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> shows</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="GW_{0,2,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N}(\alpha,\beta)=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>pt</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,2,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N}(\alpha,\beta)=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">This essentially comes from the fact in <math id="S8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for any two points there is a unique curve passing through them.
Now choose <math id="S8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{n}\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{n}\times N</annotation></semantics></math> so that its restriction to the image of <math id="S8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to a split almost complex structure. Moreover it is the standard almost complex structure on <math id="S8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(R_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(R_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Now such a <math id="S8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> might not be generic for the Gromov-Witten invariant <math id="S8.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="GW_{0,2,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N}(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>pt</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,2,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N}(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. So take a sequence of generic <math id="S8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{n}\rightarrow J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{n}\rightarrow J</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm as <math id="S8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the surfaces <math id="S8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}:=f_{i}(\{0\}\times N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}:=f_{i}(\{0\}\times N)</annotation></semantics></math>. We take a sequence of generic perturbations, so that <math id="S8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}^{n}\rightarrow\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}^{n}\rightarrow\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S8.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then the condition that <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="GW_{0,2,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N}(\alpha,\beta)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>pt</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GW_{0,2,A\times{\operatorname{pt}}}^{\mathbb{CP}^{n}(R)\times N}(\alpha,\beta)=1</annotation></semantics></math> implies there exists a nontrivial <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curve that passes through <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Now take <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, since the homology class <math id="S8.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A\times{\operatorname{pt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>pt</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\times{\operatorname{pt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive and has the lowest possible energy for <math id="S8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves, no degeneration can happen. Therefore there exists a <math id="S8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curve <math id="S8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, that passes through both <math id="S8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the monotonicity lemma to the <math id="S8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(R_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(R_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> component in the image of <math id="S8.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers <math id="S8.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}+R_{2}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}+R_{2}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
